      "text": "To find the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\), we will use the relationship between the inverse of a matrix and the powers of that matrix. Specifically, we need to find \\((\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1}\\) and use the property that \\((\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = (\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = \\mathbf{A}^{-2}\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nwe need to find \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\).\n\nFirst, let's compute \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nTo compute this, we use the matrix multiplication formula:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\begin{pmatrix}\n2 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot (-1) & 2 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot (-3) \\\\\n-1 \\cdot 2 + (-3) \\cdot (-1) & -1 \\cdot 5 + (-3) \\cdot (-3)\n\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}\n4 - 5 & 10 - 15 \\\\\n-2 + 3 & -5 + 9\n\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}\n-1 & -5 \\\\\n1 & 4\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNext, to find \\((\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1}\\), we use the fact that the inverse of a matrix \\(\\mathbf{B} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{B}^{-1} = \\frac{1}{ad - bc} \\begin{pmatrix} d & -b \\\\ -c & a \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nwhere \\((ad - bc)\\) is the determinant of the matrix.\n\nFor \\(\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\):\n\\[\n\\text{Determinant} = (-1) \\cdot 4 - (-5) \\cdot 1 = -4 + 5 = 1\n\\]\nThus, the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is:\n\\[\n(\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = \\frac{1}{1} \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
