      "text": "Because multiplying $\\mathbf{A}$ by $\\mathbf{A}^2$ is the same as applying $\\mathbf{A}$ to $\\mathbf{A}^2$, we have $\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A}^3.$ This means that $\\mathbf{A}^3 = \\mathbf{A}^2 \\cdot \\mathbf{B},$ where $\\mathbf{B} = \\mathbf{A}^{-1}.$ So, to find $\\mathbf{A}^3,$ we need to multiply $\\mathbf{A}^2$ by $\\mathbf{B}.$ $(\\mathbf{A}^2) \\cdot \\mathbf{B} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}  = \\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -7 & -15 \\\\ 7 & 9 \\end{pmatrix}}.$\n\nSort the following numbers in ascending order: $4, 4, -9, -\\frac{1}{3}, -6 \\sqrt{2}$.\n\nTo sort the numbers \\(4, 4, -9, -\\frac{1}{3}, -6 \\sqrt{2}\\) in ascending order, we need to compare their values step by step.\n\n1. **Identify the negative numbers and their relative sizes:**\n   - \\(-9\\) is clearly less than \\(-6 \\sqrt{2}\\) because \\(9\\) is greater than \\(36\\sqrt{2}\\) (since \\(\\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.414\\), and \\(36 \\times 1.414 \\approx 50.984\\)).\n   - \\(-6 \\sqrt{2}\\) is less than \\(-\\frac{1}{3}\\) because \\(-6 \\sqrt{2} \\approx -8.485\\) and \\(-\\frac{1}{3} \\approx -0.333\\).\n\n2. **Identify the positive numbers:**\n   - Both \\(4\\) numbers are equal to \\(4\\).\n\n3. **Combine all the numbers in ascending order:**\n   - The smallest number is \\(-6 \\sqrt{2}\\).\n   - The next smallest number is \\(-9\\).\n   - The next number is \\(-\\frac{1}{3}\\).\n   - The next number is \\(4\\).\n   - The largest number is \\(4\\).\n\nTherefore, the numbers in ascending order are \\(\\boxed{-6 \\sqrt{2}, -9, -\\frac{1}{3}, 4, 4}\\).",
